Instance Variables & Instance Methods :
Instance variable = a piece of data that belongs to ONE specific object,
not to every object of that class.
Instance method = a function that belongs to the class and can use/change that object's data.
class Car:
    def __init__(self, color, speed):
        self.color = color      # instance variable
        self.speed = speed      # instance variable

    def honk(self):             # instance method
        print(f"The {self.color} car says BEEP!")

car1 = Car("red", 100)
car2 = Car("blue", 80)

car1.honk()   # The red car says BEEP!
car2.honk()   # The blue car says BEEP!


Accessing Object Attributes & Methods
Once you build an object, you reach its data and actions using a dot .
Rule of thumb: object.attribute gets data, object.method() runs an action
print(car1.color)     # accessing an attribute -> red
car1.honk()            # accessing/calling a method
car1.speed = 120        # you can even change an attribute

